# .dockerignore - Docker build-context denylist for the Python health-endpoint
# tier, which serves /health on port 8000.
#
# The sibling Dockerfile builds on python:3.14-slim, copies this tier's sources
# and its declared configuration into /app, runs `python server.py`, and probes
# itself with an inline `python -c` + urllib.request HEALTHCHECK. It installs
# nothing: this composition runs on the standard library alone. Only what that
# build genuinely needs may travel into the context. Everything listed below is
# either local machine state, a build-time-only definition, or an artifact with
# no purpose inside a running container.
#
# This is an EXPLICIT DENYLIST by design. Do not rewrite it as a catch-all "*"
# followed by "!" re-inclusions: negation ordering is subtle, and one mis-ordered
# line silently drops a file the image cannot run without.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEVER ADD THESE. The first three break the build or start-up loudly. The last
# two break nothing loudly, which is exactly what makes them the dangerous ones:
#
#   server.py         The container entry point (CMD ["python", "server.py"]).
#   health.py         The payload builder and request handler; server.py imports
#                     it, so its absence is an ImportError at start-up.
#   app.py            Carries this tier's pre-existing greet() behaviour, which
#                     the feature is required to preserve, and is imported by the
#                     test suite.
#   pyproject.toml    Supplies the DECLARED name and version. health.py reads it
#                     once at import with tomllib, resolving the path from its
#                     own module directory, and never raises on failure: it
#                     records a degradation reason and serves the compiled-in
#                     literals instead. If this file never reaches the context
#                     the endpoint still answers 200 with a complete four-key
#                     body, so the HEALTHCHECK goes green and CI passes while the
#                     identity is no longer declared.
#
#                     Worse, and measured rather than assumed: the fallback
#                     literals are the SAME strings this manifest declares
#                     ("child_repo_10_LOC" and "1.0.0"), so the response body is
#                     byte-identical either way. Reading /health therefore cannot
#                     tell a declared identity from a fallback one. Only an
#                     existence check inside the image, or the one-line
#                     "health configuration degraded" notice server.py prints at
#                     start-up, distinguishes them.
#   config/health.json
#                     Supplies the declared host, port and path, with the same
#                     never-raises, fall-back-silently contract. The Dockerfile
#                     must copy it PRESERVING the subdirectory
#                     (COPY config/ ./config/).
#
#   For the same reasons, never write a broad pattern such as *.py, *.toml,
#   *.json, **/*.json, config, config/ or .python-version: each of those would
#   capture one of the files above.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Version-control metadata. Excluding it is the single highest-value entry here.
# In this repository .git is a one-line gitdir POINTER file, because this tier is
# consumed as a submodule - so it contributes no history to the context, but it
# does name a path inside the superproject's object store, and local
# remote-tracking configuration must never be baked into an image layer.
.git
.gitmodules
.gitignore

# Python execution residue. Excluding this is FUNCTIONAL, not cosmetic: bytecode
# written by a local `python server.py` or `python -m unittest` run is compiled
# for whatever interpreter happened to be on the developer's machine, and a
# stale cache entry arriving from the context can shadow a freshly copied
# source - an image serving old behaviour from new sources. The plain and the
# "**/" forms are both listed on purpose: in a .dockerignore a leading segment
# without "**" matches only at the context root, so the second form is what
# reaches config/ and any other subdirectory.
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc
**/*.pyc

# Local environment files. A machine-local .env must never be baked into an
# image layer; the .env.* form generalizes that guarantee to every dotenv
# variant (.env.local, .env.production) so a newly added one cannot slip in.
# .env.example is a documentation template read from the repository, not a
# runtime input, and is named explicitly here for the reader.
.env
.env.example
.env.*

# Documentation. Read from the repository, not from inside a container.
README.md

# Build-time-only container definitions. The daemon reads these from the context
# before the build begins; a running container has no use for either.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Continuous-integration definition. Consumed by the CI service straight from
# the repository, never from inside the image.
.github

# Test sources. The suite is exercised by CI and during development; a runtime
# image ships neither the tests nor the servers they start on ephemeral ports.
test_app.py

# The nested Java tier. It is a first-class part of this project and a TRACKED
# gitlink - it must never appear in .gitignore - but it builds its own image
# from its own Dockerfile and serves its own endpoint on port 8080. Including it
# here would ship unrelated Java sources inside this Python image.
nested_child_repo_10_LOC
